<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macehualtin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mācēhualtin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The macehualtin (singular macehualli) were the commoners of Aztec society, most of them farmers working land that belonged to their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calpulli">calpolli</a>, which a family kept as long as it did not lie fallow for more than two years. They grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans and squash and paid tribute from their produce to the nobility. Their children attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telpochcalli">telpochcalli</a> from the age of fifteen, and taking captives in war offered a commoner the one slim chance of rising into the nobility. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> (1502–1520) widened the divide, barring them from service in the royal palaces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macehualtin.jpg" alt="Macehualtin" width="320" height="686" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thelmadatter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MacehualChurubuscoDF.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most mācēhualtin were rural farmers, and on their plots they built small dams and terraces to raise their yields, projects probably organised by local communities rather than by the state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> held a locative view of the world in which everyone had a correct place: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipiltin">pipiltin</a>, the nobles, were thought made by the gods to rule, and the commoners to work for the good of society. Rituals and teachings reinforced this, as did separate sets of laws for commoners and nobles, which punished the nobility more harshly for a comparable offence.</p><p>Mobility, though uncommon, existed before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>. Commoners who rose through military deeds were called yāōtequihuah, while nobles who fell in rank were pillaquīztīltin. Commoners could also become slaves or sell their children into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Every boy received some military instruction, beginning with his father at about three years of age. Taking captives marked a man's passage to full warrior status, and failing to do so later in life brought dishonour, a heavier social blow for nobles than for commoners, though less damaging materially.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telpochcalli">telpochcalli</a>, the House of Youths, boys and girls were taught separately. Young men learned warfare and did hard physical labour, such as hauling ever larger loads of firewood as a test of strength, while young women attended the cuicalco, the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, where they learned ritual dance and song. Gender divisions began at birth: a boy's umbilical cord was given to warriors to bury on fields where battles might be fought, while a girl's was buried near the hearth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the Spanish conquest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> word mācēhualli was adopted into colonial Spanish as macehual. It was used all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> as a synonym for commoner, for subject and for native, and so outlived the Aztec order of ranks in which it had named the farmers who worked the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calpulli">calpolli</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macehualtin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aztec society, centred on the island city of Tenochtitlan, divided people first by birth into two classes: the nobles (pipiltin), perhaps one person in twenty, and the commoners (<b>macehualtin</b>).</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li></ul></section>
